---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="JWT Keys">
  <h1>JWT Keys</h1>
  <p>
    Identity signs access tokens with RS256. Production deployments must
    supply a key ring through <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS</code>. Without it the
    container generates a throwaway key on every start and logs
    <code>auto_generating_dev_rsa_key</code> as a warning.
  </p>

  <h2>Format</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS</code> is a JSON array. Each entry has a
    <code>kid</code>, a PEM-encoded RSA private key, and an
    <code>active</code> flag. Exactly one key may be active at a time —
    that is the signing key. All keys (active or not) are published to
    JWKS so previously-issued tokens continue to verify.
  </p>
  <Code code={`[
  {
    "kid": "k-2026-05",
    "private_key_pem": "-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\nMIIE...\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n",
    "active": true
  },
  {
    "kid": "k-2026-04",
    "private_key_pem": "-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\nMIIE...\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n",
    "active": false
  }
]`} lang="json" title="GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS" />
  <p>
    The parser accepts both PKCS#1 (<code>-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----</code>)
    and PKCS#8 (<code>-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----</code>) — but only RSA keys.
    See <code>parseKeyRingFromEnv</code> in <code>cmd/identity/main.go</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Generating a key</h2>
  <Code code={`# 2048-bit RSA key, PKCS#1 PEM
openssl genrsa -out k-2026-05.pem 2048

# Build the JSON value to set on the container
PEM=$(awk '{printf "%s\\\\n", $0}' k-2026-05.pem)
cat > keyring.json <<EOF
[{"kid":"k-2026-05","private_key_pem":"$PEM","active":true}]
EOF

# Pass it through env (one line, no shell expansion of \\n)
export GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS=$(cat keyring.json | jq -c .)`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>JWKS endpoint</h2>
  <p>
    Identity exposes the public half of every key in the ring at
    <code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code>. Downstream services should fetch
    this once, cache it, and use it to verify access tokens locally.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -s http://identity/.well-known/jwks.json`} lang="bash" />
  <Code code={`{
  "keys": [
    {"kty":"RSA","use":"sig","alg":"RS256","kid":"k-2026-05","n":"...","e":"AQAB"},
    {"kty":"RSA","use":"sig","alg":"RS256","kid":"k-2026-04","n":"...","e":"AQAB"}
  ]
}`} lang="json" />

  <h2>Rotation</h2>
  <p>
    See <a href="/identity/docs/examples/key-rotation">Key Rotation</a> for
    the full step-by-step. The short version:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Generate a new key with a fresh <code>kid</code>.</li>
    <li>Add it to the ring with <code>"active": false</code>; redeploy.</li>
    <li>Wait long enough for downstream services to refresh JWKS.</li>
    <li>Flip the new key to <code>active: true</code> and the old one to <code>active: false</code>; redeploy.</li>
    <li>After every old token has expired (≥ <code>GATEWAY_JWT_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code>), drop the retired key from the ring.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Verification</h2>
  <p>
    <code>VerifyAccessToken</code> in <code>pkg/jwt/token.go</code> reads the
    <code>kid</code> header, looks the key up in the ring, then verifies the
    signature, <code>exp</code>, and <code>iss</code>. Tokens with an
    unknown <code>kid</code>, expired <code>exp</code>, or wrong issuer are
    rejected.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
